# Toast Conventions

Rules for toasts that report the result of an operation (single-item or bulk). Applies to queue, conversion, import, lyrics fetch, pack install/download, and any future success/failure/other outcome toast. Keep new toast types consistent with this — do not invent per-feature formatting.

Canonical helpers: `src/lib/toastPeerOutcomes.ts`, `src/lib/toastPayloads.ts`. Toast Lab scenarios must match these rules (see §9).

## Peer outcome counters (§1)

Operations with more than one possible per-item outcome use **peer counters**. No outcome is a default/headline unless the operation can *guarantee* that counter is never zero when the toast fires. Title, color, and subtitle/button strategy derive from counters — not hand-written per scenario.

Single-subject actions (one track, or one homogeneous unit like a whole album/playlist/pack) skip this model — see §6.

## Title format (§2)

Number-first, middot-joined, zero-suppressed:

```
{count} {outcome} · {count} {outcome} · ...
```

- Render only nonzero outcomes. Never `· 0 skipped`.
- One surviving clause → that clause alone. Multiple → join with `·` (not commas).
- Number-first: `8 converted`, not `Converted 8`.
- Do **not** split a “primary” into the title and demote peers to a subtitle unless that primary is structurally guaranteed nonzero when the toast fires (import/convert do not guarantee this).

### Operation prefix (post-suppression, per toast)

- If any surviving clause uses an **operation-specific verb** (`imported`, `replaced`, `skipped`, `converted`), no prefix.
- If every surviving clause is a **generic/shared word** (`fetched`, `failed`, `not found`, `need review`), prepend `{Operation}: `.

| Scenario | Title |
|---|---|
| Convert, partial | `8 converted · 2 failed` |
| Convert, all fail | `Convert: 10 failed` |
| Import, 0 new | `5 replaced · 2 skipped` |
| Lyrics, mixed | `Lyrics: 1 fetched · 1 not found · 1 need review · 1 failed` |
| Queue, multi | `5 tracks added to queue` |

## Color (§3)

Driven only by **true error** counters (`failed`), never by “not everything succeeded”:

- `failed == 0` → success (even with not-found / skipped / need-review)
- `success > 0 && failed > 0` → warning
- `success == 0 && failed > 0` → error

## Subtitle & buttons (§4)

Use the existing primary/secondary (filled vs text) multi-action pattern when two actions are needed.

1. `need review > 0` and `failed == 0` → `[Review]` / `[Choose]` only (primary).
2. `need review > 0` and `failed >= 1` → `[Review]` primary + `[View logs]` secondary.
3. `need review == 0`:
   - `failed == 1` + **known/expected** error (tagged at throw site) → inline subtitle only, no button.
   - `failed == 1` + **generic/unhandled** → friendly inline subtitle + `[View logs]`.
   - `failed >= 2` → `[View logs]` only; never concatenate multiple error strings into the subtitle.
4. Else → no button, no subtitle.

Secondary informational breakdowns (e.g. lyrics-source provenance on import)
live in the Activity Log entry for the operation — do not add `[View logs]` on
pure success toasts just to surface them. Tag known-vs-generic at the error
throw site — toast layer reads the flag, does not infer from message text.

### Friendly copy; raw errors stay out of the toast (§4a)

- Never put raw backend / invoke / URL / exception strings in the toast **label** or **detail**.
- Toast copy is i18n’d and user-facing (e.g. “Couldn't download Xbox” / “Check your connection and try again.”).
- Put the technical message (URLs, status codes, Rust errors) in the Activity Log `message` / `details` for that operation.
- Progress toasts that fail must `settleAs` into the friendly terminal payload — do not leave the running label (e.g. “Downloading…”) with a raw `err.message` as detail.

## Activity Log (§5)

- Bulk toasts with `[View logs]` or `[Review]` must link to a corresponding Activity Log entry with full per-item detail. Do not build a separate bulk-job report surface.
- Single-subject **generic/unhandled** failures (§4.3 / §6) must also write an Activity Log entry and pass `logLink` so `[View logs]` opens it.
- Successful operations that matter for support/audit (e.g. pack install) should log provenance (`source`, registry URL, ids, version, paths) even when the success toast has no `[View logs]` button.

## Single-subject exception (§6)

```
✓ Added to queue
  {Track Title}

✓ Multiple lyrics found for Song
  [Choose]

✕ Couldn't download Xbox
  Check your connection and try again.  [View logs]
```

- Subtitle names the subject when there is exactly one coherent subject — or carries a short friendly explanation when the subject is already in the title.
- Heterogeneous multi-select: no subtitle — count stays in the title (`5 tracks added to queue`, not title/subtitle split).
- Needs-review on a single item still gets a button.
- Generic/unhandled single-subject failures **always** get `[View logs]` + an Activity Log entry (§4.3 / §5). Known/expected kinds (tagged at the throw site) stay toast-only with friendly detail.

## Zero-op (§7)

Nothing-to-do cases (e.g. “No tracks missing lyrics”) are plain info toasts — not peer-counter formatting.

## Reference shapes (§8)

```
✓ 8 converted
⚠ 8 converted · 2 failed          (+ inline if 1 known failure, else [View logs])
✕ Convert: 10 failed              [View logs]
✓ 8 imported · 2 replaced · 2 skipped
✓ Lyrics: 8 fetched
⚠ Lyrics: 1 fetched · 1 not found · 1 need review · 1 failed
  [Review]  [View logs]
✓ No tracks missing lyrics        (zero-op)
! Playback failed                 (+ [View logs] only if generic/unhandled)
✕ Couldn't download Xbox          (+ friendly detail + [View logs])
✓ Installed Xbox (1.0.0)
```

## Toast Lab (§9)

Every real production toast scenario must be registered in Toast Lab (`src/components/ToastLab/toastLabScenarios.ts`) for visual auditing.

- Add a scenario when introducing or changing a production toast (success, failure, partial, cancel, progress→settle).
- Prefer calling the same payload / `settleAs` helpers production uses (`toastPayloads.ts`, settle option builders) — do not hand-roll divergent lab-only copy.
- If the production toast includes `[View logs]`, the lab scenario must seed an Activity Log entry and pass `logLink` the same way.
- Lab button labels should name the outcome clearly (e.g. “Registry download → failed”, not only “Pack error”).
- Skipping Toast Lab for a “quick” toast is a conventions violation — treat it as part of the implementation, not optional polish.
- Remove scenarios from toast lab when they are no longer mirrored in production
